    description: "WHAT (as reported)\nWith a dependency's source traced (the remedy the inconclusive verdicts themselves suggest), verify-claims counts I/O anywhere in the traced library against a claim about the application. On Flask's tutorial app (flaskr, about 345 lines) four TRUE claims (no network calls, no subprocesses, form input never reaches a subprocess, form input never reaches the network) turned \"violated\" once Flask, Werkzeug and Click were traced. The evidence was JavaScript in Werkzeug's debugger page, Click's terminal pager, and Werkzeug's proxy middleware; flaskr calls none of them, and the subprocess paths start from no entry point at all. A claim has no way to say \"only what my code's entry points can reach\".\n\nASKED\nA decision first (owner): what a claim covers when library source is in the survey. Then the mechanism.\n\nNOT CHECKED\nWhether an existing claim field already scopes a claim to paths or entry points; ADR-0016, ADR-0047 and ADR-0061 were not re-read for it.\n\nREPORTED, NOT RE-RUN. Source: an outside reviewer who tried hypergumbo 10.0.0 from PyPI on 2026-11-24 (transcript pasted by the owner in session). Nothing in this item was run on dev by the filing agent unless a line says so; the reviewer's fixtures are not on this machine. FIRST STEP: reproduce on current dev with a fixture, and check the unreleased changelog, before building anything. Filed at the owner's request (\"file them all now\")."  # 712f
